    "title": "Tennessee Basketball's Rick Barnes Affirms That He's 100% Committed To Coaching Next Season | Rocky Top Insider",
    "text": "Photo via Tennessee Athletics CHICAGO — Tennessee head coach Rick Barnes will return for a 12th season with the Vols and for his 40th season as a head coach, he told the media following the Vols’ 95-62 season-ending loss against Michigan in the Elite Eight. Asked if he was 100% committed to being back at Tennessee next season, Barnes kept it simple.Why a Giants-Odell Bechkam Jr. reunion could work out | Blue Rush “Yeah, I am,” Barnes said postgame. Barnes just completed his 11th season as Tennessee’s head coach, leading the Vols to the Elite Eight for a third straight season. But much like last season against Houston, Tennessee posed little threat for the top-seed Wolverines in the Midwest Regional final. The Hickory, North Carolina native has led Tennessee to its best era in program history. In his first 11 years as head coach, Tennessee has made the five Sweet 16 appearances and three Elite Eight appearances. Prior to Barnes tenure, the Vols had made the Sweet 16 just seven times and the Elite Eight just once. More From RTI: What’s Next For Tennessee Basketball Following Third Straight Elite Eight Loss Tennessee had never made the Sweet 16 three consecutive years prior to Barnes’ arrival but have now made the second weekend of the tournament four straight times. However, the Final Four continues to elude a Tennessee program that’s won more NCAA Tournament games than any other without a trip to the final weekend of the tournament. Barnes has one Final Four appearance in his career, reaching the national semifinal in 2003 at Texas. “We want more,” Barnes said. “There’s no doubt we want more. We want to break through this thing. But you’ve got to get here first. Just so proud of the effort these guys gave it this year.” The 2025-26 Tennessee basketball team making it to the Elite Eight was a much bigger surprise then the previous two years. Tennessee lost 11 games entering the NCAA Tournament and earned a six-seed— its lowest during Barnes tenure. But Tennessee played its best basketball at the right time of year, defeating Miami (Ohio), Virginia and Iowa State on its way to the Elite Eight before losing to Michigan. The Vols lose Ja’Kobi Gillespie and Felix Okpara to graduation while also likely losing Nate Ament to the NBA Draft. Tennessee will likely lose and add players from the portal but will look to build around its young core including JP Estrella, Bishop Boswell, Amari Evans and DeWayne Brown.",
